Support drawing a border inside a reserved, immutable Margin, without
letting that concern grow into the thin ncurses wrapper.

The functionality is split over two classes:

1. terminal::BasicWindow: the current thin wrapper around a single
   ncurses WINDOW*. It exists so that the rest of the code can use
   ncurses without including curses.h, and adds no semantics of its own;
   it keeps doing exactly that under a new name (rename done in goal
   02.5).

2. terminal::Window: a window that may have a Margin - reserved
   top/bottom/left/right cell rows/columns (see the drawing in Margin.h)
   in which its border is drawn - but doesn't have to. Window derives
   from BasicWindow (spelled `using InnerWindow = BasicWindow;`): every
   inherited member function operates on the writable area inside the
   margin, so writes stay inside it without repeating member functions.
   Additionally Window has a private BasicWindow outer_window_ - the
   margin-inclusive window the border is drawn on. Internally the
   writable area is an ncurses subwindow inset by the margin, but that
   is an implementation detail the API never reveals; without a margin
   no subwindow is created at all. The margin is immutable: fixed when
   the Window is created; changing it means creating a new Window.

(The name MarginWindow was considered for class 2 but rejected: a
Window without a margin is not a "margin window".)
